title: Mass writes in the store
tags: Storage
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A write to many records files one pending index write per operation in the field's list, and one pending write per part of the ids, instead of a write per record; reads replay them, a fold writes them in. The log of such a write is one entry too ([[Mass operations in the log]]).

Setting a tag on ten thousand files (`mf … field set|add|remove` on a query,
[[Query endpoints]]) is one write to many records. Indexing it record by record has
two costs. Filing each record under the value it gains is cheap — the holders of one
value are neighbours in the store. Taking each record ''out'' of the value it loses is
not: that value has to be known, so every record is read first.

!! Past a threshold only

A write to many records goes in bulk only when it would change more records than the
repository's ''mass-write threshold'' — `[settings] mass-write-threshold` in the
daemon's configuration (20 shipped), overridden by `mass_write_threshold` in the
repository's `config.json` ([[Repository layout on disk]]). The records it would
change are known before anything is written, from the postings and the counters: an
add counts the records lacking the value, a remove those holding it, an unset those
holding the field, a set every record. At or under the threshold, those records are
written one at a time, as a write to each alone would be — nothing is left pending,
and no read pays for it later. A deletion of many records is held to the same
threshold ([[Mass operations in the log]]).

The threshold is a starting value: one fixed number cannot be right for every
operation and every disk (the costs measured are in `notes-performance-stockage.txt`,
0quater), and a way to derive it from the repository is to come. A writer the
repository did not configure (a test's, an init's) has a threshold of 0: every such
write in bulk.

!! The write clock

Everything ordered against the pending writes takes a number from one clock,
`next_write_number` in `global_primary_state` ([[Store tables]]): a pending write, a
pending index write, a field or a value written by hand on a record a pending write
names. It is never set back. Only the order of the numbers matters.

!! Pending index writes

An ''add'' or a ''removal'' to many records goes straight into the value's posting —
`∪ B` or `∖ B`, one key per chunk of B, no record read — in every chunk where the field
has nothing pending: nearly always (user decision, November 2026). A ''set'' or an
''unset'' cannot: it would have to take B out of every value the records held, which only
reading them, or every value of the field, tells. It waits for the fold.

A field has a list of ''pending index writes'' (table `index_pending_writes`, keyed by
field, chunk and write number). A set or an unset to many records appends one, naming the
records — a bitmap of dense ids — and files nothing per record; so does an add or a
removal in a chunk where one is pending, behind it. It is filed ''by chunk'' of 65 536 ids,
as the postings are: one key per chunk it touches, under one write number, each holding
the ids of its chunk. Chunks name disjoint ids, so replaying a field's keys chunk by
chunk is replaying them, for each id, in the order they were written:

|!Kind |!Content |!A value's holders become |
|add |type · value |`∪ B` for the value |
|remove |type · value |`∖ B` for the value |
|set |type · values (one or more) |`∖ B`, then `∪ B` for the values given |
|unset |— |`∖ B` |

A set or an unset never names the values it removes. `nothing` is the exception: its
holders are a posting with a key of its own, known in advance, so the write itself
keeps it exact — a set or an unset takes B out of it, a set to `nothing` puts B in —
and it is never replayed nor folded. An add of a value over records holding `nothing`
is a set on them (`Writer::add_over_nothing`), and an add of `nothing` over records
holding values one too: a field holds `nothing` or values, never both
([[Nothing versus unknown]]). The holders of a value
are what is ''stored'' — its posting in `index_value_holders`, which for a `ref` are its
referrers ([[Store tables]]) — with the field's pending index writes replayed over it,
in order.

What cannot wait is kept exact at once, by set operations whatever the number of
records: the holders of `nothing`, the count of values of each record (an increment on a set of ids is a carry
from bit to bit, [[Store tables]]), `live_metarecords`, the long-text sets, the
trigrams ([[Text search in the store]]: a new text's entries by value, or the ids
pending by metarecord), and the histograms for a value added or removed.

!! What reads do

* ''Equality, referrers, a text search by value'': the value's stored holders, replayed.
* ''A range, a `!=`, a scan'': the values a pending index write names are left out of the scan of what is stored and read one by one, replayed; every other value has only lost the records a set or an unset was written on, which are subtracted once.
* ''A sort by walking the values'' ([[Sorting and postings]]): the values a pending index write names are stepped over where they are stored and handed over at their place in the order, their holders replayed — a value no record held before included; the other values come as stored, less the records set or unset. A page still costs the page (`tests/kv_events.rs`: 11 067 keys for a page of 50 over 3 000 records when such a page was fetched from the rows, under 1 000 walked). Only a named value whose key does not hold it whole — a text over 256 bytes, written to one record while writes were pending — has no place in the walk: the page is then fetched from the rows.

A field without pending index writes — nearly all of them, nearly always — is read as
before, for one read of its (empty) list.

!! Order

While a field has pending index writes in a chunk, a write there — to ''one'' record, or
an add or a removal to many — is one too: written into what is stored, it would be
overwritten by the replay of an older one. In the other chunks it is written in. The counts and the sets above are kept at each write, in order, and never
replayed.

!! The fold

`KvStore::fold` — and a reindex, and the idle fold ([[Folding in idle time]]) — writes
the pending index writes into what is stored, one chunk of a field at a time
(`fold_events_chunk`): the field's postings of that chunk are read, every pending
index write of the chunk is replayed over them in memory, oldest first, they are
written back and the chunk's pending index writes deleted. A set or an unset is where
its removed values are found: it names ids, not values, so every value the field has
in the chunk — a walk of the keys of its postings, not of their bitmaps — loses the
ids it was written on, and the histograms give back the rows they held; the values it
adds, like an add or a removal, were counted when it was written. Since chunks name
disjoint ids, a field folded in some chunks and not in others reads right. Until then the histogram of a field with pending index writes counts those rows,
so `mf repo check` does not hold it to the rows; the postings are held to them as
replayed ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]).

!! The rows: one pending write per part

The pending index writes say, for a value, which records hold it. The ''rows'' of a
record say the other way round, which values it holds, and they are not written
either: a write to many records appends, to the list of every ''part'' of the ids it
touches — 64 k ids, the high bits of the dense id — one ''pending write'' (table
`pending_writes`, keyed by part and write number): its kind, its content and the ids
of that part. A mass write thus writes as many keys as it has parts, and no record.

|!Kind |!Content |!Applied to a record |
|add |field · type · value |puts the value in, and takes a `nothing` out |
|remove |field · type · value |takes the value out |
|set |field · type · values (one or more) |the field's values become these |
|unset |field |the field goes |
|delete |the op id of the lowest id it took in the part, and the ids it took there (the op of an id is its rank among them, whatever the bitmap lost since) |nothing: it marks the moment whose rows are the before of the deletion (below) |

The kinds are the effects of the log ([[Log storage]]). The ids of a pending write
are live when it is appended — they are taken from `live_metarecords` —, which the
code asserts.

A record is read as what it holds, with the pending writes of its part that name it
applied in the order of their numbers. Reading a record costs the pending writes of
its part, which is why the idle fold writes them in ([[Folding in idle time]]) — a list
has no limit of its own, and [[Storage wear]] says how long they are. When no part has
any, nothing is looked for.

!! Written by hand meanwhile

The most recent write wins, a write to one record included. A record that a pending
write names carries, after its fields, when it was last written ''by hand''
([[Store tables]]):

* ''fixed fields'': a field and a write number — the field set or unset by hand;
* ''fixed values'': a field, a value and a write number — the value added or removed by hand; whether it is there, the rows say.

A pending write older than the number does not touch that field, or that value. A
fixed field makes the older fixed values of its field useless, and they go; all of
them go when no pending write names the record any more. The rows carry no number.
Such a write loads the record with the pending writes applied, changes it, and writes
the result: the pending writes stay in the list and apply to it again, harmlessly. A
record no pending write names is written as ever, without extras.

!! A deletion of many records

A deletion of many records is a pending write too, of kind `delete`, under its write
number like the others. The records stay as they were: a `delete` marks the moment
whose rows are the ''before'' of the deletion, which its log entry needs to be undone
([[Mass operations in the log]]). `live_metarecords` — and the counts, the parents —
lose the ids at once.

* ''Its fold'', record by record, copies the rows read at that moment into the before of its log entry, chunk by chunk ([[Log storage]]), and, if the record is still deleted, takes its rows out of the histograms.
* ''An undo of the deletion before its fold'' puts the ids back in `live_metarecords` and the indexes kept at once; the `delete` stays until its fold, since it is what says that the before is still in the record.
* ''A write to a record that a `delete` not folded names'' folds it for that record first: the id leaves the `delete`'s bitmap, the before is written.

There is no kind of its own for a restoration: no pending write can name a deleted id,
so nothing between a `delete` and the restoration is to be set aside.

!! The fold of the rows

The fold (`KvStore::fold`, a reindex, and the idle fold of [[Folding in idle time]],
one list at a time) writes the pending writes into the records of their part and
empties the list; a metarecord that is gone is not written back. No list is folded
for being long: a fold set off by a size would fall in the middle of the user's work,
a slowdown at random (user decision).

The log of a write to many records is one entry as well ([[Mass operations in the log]]);
no record is read to write it.

Positions (`tree_ref`, `hierarchy_ref`) are never part of a pending index write: each
is written on its own ([[One position per forest]]). A `nothing` added or removed is
a pending index write like any value; it is not counted, the counts being of values.
A text past its key's length (256 bytes) is a posting like any value, under its cut
key and hash ([[Store tables]]). The one write left to go one record at a time is the
''removal'' of a text too long for trigrams (over 512 bytes): the long-text set says
which records hold such a text, and whether another stays on a record cannot be known
without reading it ([[Text search in the store]]); its addition, and a set, set it
exactly.

`tests/kv_events.rs` holds every reader to the oracle with writes pending, after a
fold and after a reindex.
